<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/misconception> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#Misconception> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/misconception> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#fact> "Congress called the meeting only to revise the Articles of Confederation and report proposed alterations to Congress and the states; the delegates decided on their own, by mid-June 1787, to draft an entirely new frame of government, and their Constitution took effect once nine state conventions had ratified it." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/misconception> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#myth> "The delegates were sent to Philadelphia to write a new constitution." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/misconception> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#why> "Because the convention's product replaced the Articles completely and is commemorated on September 17, it is easy to assume a new constitution was the assignment from the start, which hides how bold, and how contested, the delegates' decision was." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#ReviewQuestion> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Why did the convention end up writing a whole new constitution when Congress had asked only for revisions?" .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/1> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#answer> "Many delegates believed the Articles left the central government too weak; Randolph's Virginia Plan on May 29 proposed a new national legislature, executive and judiciary, and by mid-June the delegates had accepted that they were drafting a new frame of government rather than amending the old one." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/1> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#ReviewQuestion> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "How did the Great Compromise settle the quarrel between large and small states?" .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/2> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#answer> "It gave the House of Representatives seats in proportion to each state's population and gave every state equal representation in the Senate; it passed on July 16, 1787, by a single state's margin." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/2> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#ReviewQuestion> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "How did the convention's decisions strengthen slaveholding states?" .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/3> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#answer> "Counting three-fifths of enslaved people added to slave states' seats in the House and, through the electoral college, their weight in presidential elections; the convention also barred Congress from ending the Atlantic slave trade before 1808 and added a fugitive slave clause." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/3> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#ReviewQuestion> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/4> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Why do historians disagree about whether the Constitution was a proslavery document?" .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/4> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#answer> "Paul Finkelman argues its many protections for slavery made it a proslavery bargain, while Sean Wilentz argues that by refusing to recognise property in people the framers left an opening later antislavery politicians used; critics of Wilentz note that his key Madison quotation may have been added to Madison's notes later." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/review/4> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/timeline/10> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#TimelineEntry> .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/timeline/10> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Congress unanimously resolves to send the Constitution to the states for ratifying conventions." .
<https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/constitutional-convention/timeline/10> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/ontology#date> "1787-09-28"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
